{
  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "gauss-1814",
      "why": "Original derivation: chooses n nodes as zeros of the n-th Legendre polynomial and shows exactness up to degree 2n-1 and optimality."
    },
    {
      "id": "jacobi-1826",
      "why": "Re-derives Gauss's rule via orthogonality and proves the node polynomial must be orthogonal to lower-degree polynomials and degree 2n is impossible."
    },
    {
      "id": "legendre-1785",
      "why": "Establishes the orthogonality, three-term recurrence, and n distinct real zeros of P_n, which justify using those zeros as nodes."
    },
    {
      "id": "trefethen-2013",
      "why": "Modern treatment including the proof that an n-point rule cannot be exact for the square of its node polynomial and so cannot reach degree 2n."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Gauss introduced the n-point quadrature rule with nodes at the zeros of the n-th Legendre polynomial and showed it integrates every polynomial up to degree 2n-1 exactly and is optimal among n-node rules [gauss-1814]. Jacobi recast this via orthogonality of the node polynomial to all lower-degree polynomials, identifying the nodes as the Legendre zeros and demonstrating degree 2n cannot be attained [jacobi-1826]; Legendre's work supplies the required orthogonality and zero-location properties of P_n [legendre-1785]. Modern expositions give concise proofs of the impossibility to reach degree 2n by showing the rule annihilates the positive integral of the square of the node polynomial [trefethen-2013]."
}
